  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain associated with the remnants of Hurricane\nIsaias arrives today, along with gusty non-thunderstorm winds.\n\nRainfall will start across portions of northwest North Carolina\nby mid morning, and continue to spread north and eastward to \nencompass the remainder of the area by the afternoon and early \nevening hours. In general, much of the forecast area is likely \nto see 1-2 inches of rainfall, with higher totals expected along\nand east of the Blue Ridge due to upslope flow, and lower \ntotals west of the Blue Ridge given a rain shadow effect. See \nthe Hydro section below for more details on the flooding \npotential.\n\nA very strong 850mb LLJ is expected to lead to a downslope wind\nevent for areas in SE West Virginia, SW Virginia, and NW North\nCarolina. HREF probabilities of 50%-80% for winds gusting to \ngreater than 45 mph across this western corridor of the CWA from\nNW North Carolina into SE West Virginia. Wind Advisory still \nseems suitable for these areas and no upgrade or expansion to \nthe current wind headlines is expected. May still have a few \nhigher gusts above 3500ft reaching close to high wind warning \ncriteria, but these should be intermittent and isolated. Wind \ngusts should diminish by late Sunday morning and into the \nafternoon as the LLJ lifts northeast out of the region.\n\nBy Monday, all remnants of Isaias will be well east of the \nregion with just some lingering wrap around upslope showers for\nthe western slopes through late Monday morning. Dry through most\nof the upcoming week with possibly the next chance of rain by the\nend of next week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Damaging winds and tornado threat on Sunday.\n\nBy Sunday afternoon, hi-res guidance is still showing high \npressure retreating enough to allow for a brief period of \ndestabilization along a triple point in central Virginia/north\ncentral North Carolina, which could pose a brief tornado \nthreat. Strong Theta-E advection on the RRFS/NAM guidance shows \nthis potential rather well. Will not take much destablization to\nsupport rotating low- topped supercells given 0-1km SRH \nexceeding 200m2s2 and Bulk Shear 40-50kts. Still a lot of\nuncertainty given continued precipitation and clouds ongoing on\nSunday in the warm sector, which could limit daytime heating \nand destabilization.",
